From: Andrea Campanella <emuboy85@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ata: pata_ps2: PIO only unless dma=1

The DMA transfers run on the IOP while the main CPU reads the ATA
registers of the interface. With a fast disk (a SATA SSD on a bridge)
the main CPU reads the status register while the DMA still holds the
interface, and gets a data bus error in ata_sff_check_status during a
write. PIO moves the data through the main CPU and has no such race.

Leave the DMA modes off unless the module parameter dma=1 is given.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [PlayStation 2 port]

--- a/drivers/ata/pata_ps2.c
+++ b/drivers/ata/pata_ps2.c
@@ -27,6 +27,15 @@
 
 #define DRV_NAME "pata-ps2"
 
+/*
+ * DMA runs on the IOP while the main CPU reads the ATA registers, and with
+ * a fast disk the two meet: a bus error on the status register during a
+ * write. PIO is safe. dma=1 turns the DMA modes on.
+ */
+static bool pata_ps2_dma;
+module_param_named(dma, pata_ps2_dma, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dma, "Use the DMA modes (default: PIO only)");
+
 #define MAX_ATA_SIF_SG (SIF_CMD_PACKET_DATA_MAX / sizeof(struct ata_sif_payload_sg))
 
 /**
@@ -395,8 +404,10 @@
 
 	ap->ops = &pata_ps2_port_ops;
 	ap->pio_mask   = ATA_PIO4;
-	ap->mwdma_mask = ATA_MWDMA2;
-	ap->udma_mask  = ATA_UDMA4;	// FIXME: ATA_UDMA5?
+	if (pata_ps2_dma) {
+		ap->mwdma_mask = ATA_MWDMA2;
+		ap->udma_mask  = ATA_UDMA4;	// FIXME: ATA_UDMA5?
+	}
 	ap->flags     |= ATA_FLAG_NO_ATAPI;
 
 	pp->dev = &pdev->dev;
